  "body_html": "<h1>Matching Market (One-to-One)</h1>\n<p>A <strong>one-to-one matching market</strong> consists of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A finite nonempty set $M$ of <em>men</em> (proposers).</li>\n<li>A finite nonempty set $W$ of <em>women</em> (receivers).</li>\n<li>For each $i \\in M$, a strict linear order $\\succ_i$ over $W \\cup \\{\\bot_i\\}$,\n  where $\\bot_i$ represents staying unmatched.</li>\n<li>For each $j \\in W$, a strict linear order $\\succ_j$ over $M \\cup \\{\\bot_j\\}$.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We say $j$ is <strong>acceptable</strong> to $i$ if $j \\succ_i \\bot_i$ (and symmetrically for\n$i$ acceptable to $j$). The market makes no use of cardinal utilities — only\nthe ordinal preferences matter.</p>\n<p>In Lean this is the <code>MatchingMarket</code> structure under\n<code>EconCSLib.MarketDesign.Matching.Basic</code>. The \"two-sided\" terminology refers to\nthe two disjoint participant sets $M$, $W$; \"one-to-one\" refers to each\nparticipant being matched to at most one partner.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MSZ Ch.22, Def 22.1] Maschler, Solan, Zamir, <em>Game Theory</em>.</li>\n</ul>",
